Here's a possible implementation of a public class with private methods using encapsulation:

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private String accountNumber;
    private double balance;

    // Private constructor to prevent instantiation from outside
    private BankAccount(String accountNumber, double initialBalance) {
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
            System.out.println("Deposited: " + amount);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid deposit amount");
        }
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    // Private method to withdraw money
    private void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0 && amount <= balance) {
            balance -= amount;
            System.out.println("Withdrew: " + amount);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid withdrawal amount");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BankAccount account = new BankAccount("123456789", 1000.0);
        account.deposit(500.0);
        account.withdraw(200.0);
        System.out.println(account.getBalance());
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the `balance` variable is private and can only be accessed through the `getBalance()` method. The `withdraw()` method also has access to the `balance`, but it must use a private name (`_amount`) to avoid polluting the public interface with its internal implementation details.
